MAJGOJGM_05308	234621.RER_12700	2.95e-52	164.0	COG3369@1|root,COG3369@2|Bacteria,2GUHK@201174|Actinobacteria	201174|Actinobacteria	S	Identified by match to SM00705 CDGSH-type zinc finger domain. Zinc finger (Znf) domains are relatively small protein motifs that bind one or more zinc atoms, and which usually contain multiple finger-like protrusions that make tandem contacts with their target molecule. They were first identified as a DNA-binding motif in transcription factor TFIIIA from Xenopus laevis, however they are now recognised to bind DNA, RNA, protein and or lipid substrates. The CDGSH-type zinc finger contains a CDGSH sequence motif (although the Ser residue can also be an Ala or Thr), and is found in proteins from a wide range of organisms with the exception of fungi. Proteins carrying this domain include ferredoxin-dependent glutamate synthase. CDGSH-type domains are also found in the iron-containing outer membrane protein mitoNEET. MitoNEET contains the conserved sequence C-X-C-X2-(S T)-X3-P-X-C-D-G-(S A T)-H, a defining feature of CDGSH domains, and is likely involved in iron binding rather than zinc binding	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	-	zf-CDGSH
